  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "/c96mqPK70zzSQVeS/2iRQQv6Ig=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:25:57.639Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "IG&H was a top pick in the v2 ranking and is now filed do-not-pitch. The vertical fit did not change and is still described as excellent; what changed is that OutSystems Premier status was recognised as slot occupancy. The app layer a Powerhouse engagement would occupy is already held by a proprietary competitor, and Premier is the deepest tier of that relationship rather than a casual one.\n\nThis is the clearest example in the cohort of one criterion overriding all the others. Vertical fit, size and geography are the attributes that make a lead pleasant to work with; slot occupancy decides whether there is anything to sell. A Premier partner has certification investment, co-selling motions and pipeline attached to the incumbent platform, so a competing layer arrives as a threat to the practice rather than an addition to it.\n\nThe residual value recorded is conversion at best \u2014 the same demotion path as ITSector in Portugal. That reframes the engagement from partnership to displacement, which needs a different pitch, a different sponsor inside the firm, and a much longer horizon than the current pipeline supports.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**9 \u00b7 do not pitch**\n- IG&H (NL \u00b7 mid)\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> WAS a top pick in v2. Excellent vertical fit, but OutSystems Premier means your slot is filled by a proprietary competitor. Conversion target at best.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:25:57.639893Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:25:57.639896Z"
    }
  },
